      "The placebo effect also has a lesser-known counterpart called the nocebo effect, in which negative expectations produce genuinely harmful symptoms, such as patients experiencing real pain after being warned, incorrectly, that a treatment might hurt. Together, these phenomena illustrate just how deeply expectation shapes bodily experience, complicating the design of clinical trials, since researchers must account for the fact that even the act of informing patients about possible side effects can partly cause those very side effects.",
